psychologists of the 20th century. Welcome, Dr. Loftus. Thank you. It's wonderful to have you here today. My pleasure. So your research tells us something I would think is unsettling about our minds, that our memories aren't set in stone, that they're basically subject to manipulation. So can you explain that a bit more? One of the things that I and other people who do similar work have shown is that once you have an experience and you record it in memory, it doesn't just stick there in some pristine form, you know, waiting to be played back like a recording device, but rather new information, new ideas, new thoughts, suggestive information, misinformation, can enter people's conscious

